<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Love song · Lament · 1954 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">Cucurrucucú paloma</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Mexican song of 1954 whose refrain copies the soft call of the mourning dove.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Tomás Méndez</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Tomás Méndez</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Mexican</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Mexico</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1954</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Love song · Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (words and music by Tomás Méndez, written 1954)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cucurrucucu-paloma">Cucurrucucú paloma</a>&quot; (&quot;Coo-coo dove&quot;) is a Mexican song in the huapango style, written by Tomás Méndez in 1954. Its title imitates the call of the mourning dove, which its refrain echoes. It was first heard in the Mexican comedy film Escuela de vagabundos (1955), sung by the film's star, Pedro Infante, and it gave its name to Miguel Delgado's film Cucurrucucú Paloma (1965), in which Lola Beltrán sang it in the role of &quot;Paloma Méndez&quot;. Its first release on record was a version sung by Harry Belafonte in 1956.</p><p>The song has since travelled far beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. It has been recorded by singers from Luis Miguel, Rocío Dúrcal, Miguel Aceves Mejía and Lila Downs to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthew-c-perry">Perry</a> Como, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanaya">Nana</a> Mouskouri, Julio <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iglesias">Iglesias</a>, Hibari Misora and Joan Baez, and Franco Battiato took up its refrain in his own song &quot;Cuccurucucù&quot;. It is heard in many films, among them The Last Sunset (1961), Happy Together (1997) and Moonlight (2016); in Pedro Almodóvar's Talk to Her (2002) the Brazilian singer Caetano Veloso sings it as an art song, far from the mariachi style of its first appearance on screen. In 2019 the tenor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-diego">Juan Diego</a> Flórez sang it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-scala">La Scala</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, accompanying himself on the guitar, in a performance watched 17 million times on YouTube.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of lovesickness, sorrowful and yearning, built around the mourning dove's call.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so they are not printed here. &#8220;What it says&#8221; above gives their theme in our own words, and the performance below lets you hear them.</p><div class="sg-genius" data-genius="764979"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn">Show the lyrics from Genius</button><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press the button. <a href="https://genius.com/Lola-beltran-cucurrucucu-paloma-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a>.</p></div></section>
